Kīlauea, on the Island of Hawaiʻi, is one of the most
active volcanoes on Earth. On April 30, 2018, the floor of its long-lived Puʻu ʻŌʻō vent
collapsed, and magma began moving downrift toward the Puna District. Ground cracks
opened in and around the Leilani Estates subdivision on May 2, and the first fissure
eruptions began on May 3. A magnitude 6.9 earthquake struck the volcano’s south flank
the following day. Over the next three months, 24 fissures opened along the lower East
Rift Zone. One of them, Fissure 8, produced a channelized lava river that reached the
coast and filled Kapoho Bay in early June, evaporating the largest natural freshwater lake
in Hawaiʻi on its way. At the summit, the draining magma reservoir caused the caldera
floor to collapse repeatedly, deepening Halemaʻumaʻu crater. The eruption ended on
August 4, 2018. It covered 13.7 square miles of land, destroyed more than 700 homes, created roughly 875 acres of new land where lava entered the ocean, and generated
about 60,000 earthquakes. The Landsat 8 images in this story show that landscape
before, during, and six months after.

